diff --git a/docs/self-hosting.md b/docs/self-hosting.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1a4043e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/self-hosting.md
@@ -0,0 +1,117 @@
+## Self-hosting
+
+Fizzy ships with a Kamal configuration that runs the whole app on a single box: one Docker container for the web app and its background jobs, SQLite for its data, and SSL terminated by [kamal-proxy](https://kamal-deploy.org/docs/proxy). Someone with a Linux server can go from a fork to Fizzy at `https://their-host` with nothing but Kamal.
+
+Everything lives in [`config/deploy.yml`](../config/deploy.yml) and `.kamal/secrets.example`. `bin/kamal` reads the config, builds the image with the [`Dockerfile`](../Dockerfile) and deploys it.
+
+
+### What you need
+
+- A Linux server with SSH access as root, 2 GB of RAM or more, and 20 GB of disk. It doesn't need Docker installed: Kamal installs it the first time you deploy.
+- A hostname with a DNS record pointing at the server, and ports 80 and 443 reachable. kamal-proxy answers the Let's Encrypt challenge on 80 and serves traffic on 443.
+- An account on a container registry the image can be pushed to (Docker Hub, GHCR, a self-hosted one). Kamal builds the image on the machine you deploy from, so that machine needs Docker too.
+- A mail server Fizzy can send through. Sign-in is by magic link, so mail has to work before anybody can get in.
+
+
+### 1. Fill in `config/deploy.yml`
+
+| What | Where |
+| --- | --- |
+| Your hostname | `servers.web.hosts` and `proxy.host` |
+| The image to build and push | `image` and `registry.username` |
+| Your mail server | `env.clear.SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_USERNAME` |
+| The from address for mail | `env.clear.MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` |
+
+Two settings are worth knowing about even though they are optional:
+
+- `APP_HOST` -- links in emails, notifications and webhooks are built from the public hostname. It defaults to the host Kamal deployed to, so only set it when that differs from the hostname people visit.
+- `MULTI_TENANT: "true"` -- lets anybody sign up for an account of their own. Without it, Fizzy runs a single account and people join it with a join code.
+
+
+### 2. Fill in the secrets
+
+```sh
+cp .kamal/secrets.example .kamal/secrets
+```
+
+Then fill in:
+
+- `KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD` -- an access token for the registry. Kamal uses it to push the image and the box uses it to pull it.
+- `SECRET_KEY_BASE` -- signs sessions and cookies. Generate one with `openssl rand -hex 64` and keep it: losing it signs everybody out.
+- `SMTP_PASSWORD` -- the password for your mail server. Leave it empty if your relay accepts unauthenticated mail.
+- `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` and `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` -- only if you want browser push notifications. Generate a pair with `bin/rails runner 'key = WebPush.generate_key; puts "VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{key.public_key}"; puts "VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{key.private_key}"'`.
+
+`.kamal/secrets` is gitignored. Keep real values there and out of the repository.
+
+
+### 3. Deploy
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+Kamal installs Docker on the box, boots kamal-proxy, builds and pushes the image, prepares the database and starts the container. After that, visit `https://your-host` and sign in with your email address: the first account to sign up owns it, and everybody else joins from the join code in its settings.
+
+Subsequent deploys are the same thing without the setup:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+Migrations run when the new container boots, so an update is a `git pull` and a deploy. If it goes wrong, `bin/kamal rollback` puts the previous release back.
+
+If mail is not set up yet, you can still get in: the sign-in code is in the database. Read it from the box and type it into the page you are stuck on:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal console
+# and then:
+MagicLink.last.code
+```
+
+
+### What runs where
+
+| What | Where |
+| --- | --- |
+| Web app, Solid Queue workers, Solid Cache, Solid Cable | the `fizzy-web` container |
+| App database, queue, cache, cable databases | `fizzy_storage` volume, `/rails/storage` |
+| Uploaded files | `fizzy_storage` volume, `/rails/storage/production/files` |
+| SSL, HTTP, routing | the `kamal-proxy` container, ports 80 and 443 |
+
+There is nothing to keep alive outside Docker: Solid Queue runs inside Puma, so background jobs run on the same box as the web app.
+
+
+### Looking after it
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal app logs -f   # tail the application log
+bin/kamal console       # a Rails console on the box
+bin/kamal shell         # a shell in the container
+bin/kamal doctor        # check the deployment is healthy
+```
+
+
+### Backups
+
+Everything that matters is in the `fizzy_storage` volume, so archiving it is a backup. From the machine you deploy from:
+
+```sh
+ssh root@your-host 'docker run --rm -v fizzy_storage:/data -v "$PWD":/backup alpine tar czf /backup/fizzy-storage-$(date +%F).tgz -C /data .'
+```
+
+SQLite is being written while the archive is taken, so for a copy you can be sure about, stop the app first and start it again after:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal app stop
+# ... take the archive ...
+bin/kamal app start
+```
+
+Restoring is the same archive in reverse: put the files back into the volume and deploy.
+
+
+### Going further
+
+- Fizzy works with MySQL too: set `DATABASE_ADAPTER=mysql` and point the `MYSQL_*` variables at your database (see `config/database.mysql.yml`).
+- Uploaded files can go to S3 instead of the volume: set `ACTIVE_STORAGE_SERVICE=s3` and the `S3_*` variables (see `config/storage.oss.yml`).
+- `saas/` is 37signals' companion gem for the hosted version. It is not needed to run Fizzy yourself, but its deploy configuration may be useful if you outgrow one box.
diff --git a/test/deploy-config-test b/test/deploy-config-test
new file mode 100755
index 0000000..7aa0bc6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/deploy-config-test
@@ -0,0 +1,108 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+# The single-box Kamal deployment is the only way third parties run Fizzy, so
+# this pins the shape of config/deploy.yml: one web host behind an SSL proxy, a
+# volume for everything Fizzy writes, a registry to push to, a mail server, and
+# secrets that .kamal/secrets.example documents. It also checks the guide and
+# the gitignore still agree with the config.
+#
+# Hermetic: no network, no Docker, no SSH. Kamal itself is not needed because
+# the config is rendered the way Kamal renders it -- ERB, then YAML.
+#
+# Invocation (from anywhere):
+#
+#   test/deploy-config-test
+set -uo pipefail
+
+ROOT="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)"
+DEPLOY="$ROOT/config/deploy.yml"
+SECRETS="$ROOT/.kamal/secrets.example"
+GUIDE="$ROOT/docs/self-hosting.md"
+
+pass=0 fail=0
+ok()  { pass=$((pass+1)); echo "  ok: $1"; }
+bad() { fail=$((fail+1)); echo "  FAIL: $1"; }
+
+# Render the config the way Kamal does: ERB first, then YAML.
+rendered="$(ruby -rerb -ryaml -e 'puts YAML.dump(YAML.unsafe_load(ERB.new(File.read(ARGV[0]), trim_mode: "-").result) || {})' "$DEPLOY" 2>&1)"
+if [ $? -ne 0 ]; then
+  bad "config/deploy.yml renders"
+  echo "$rendered" >&2
+  echo "1 failure, aborting." >&2
+  exit 1
+fi
+
+assert() { # assert <description> <expression over the rendered config>
+  local desc="$1" expression="$2"
+  if ruby -ryaml -e '
+    config = YAML.safe_load(STDIN.read, aliases: true)
+    abort "nope" unless (eval(ARGV[0]) rescue false)
+  ' "$expression" <<<"$rendered" 2>/dev/null; then
+    ok "$desc"
+  else
+    bad "$desc"
+  fi
+}
+
+assert "declares the fizzy service"                'config["service"] == "fizzy"'
+assert "names an image to build and push"          'config["image"].to_s =~ %r{\A[^/]+/[^/]+\z}'
+assert "deploys to exactly one web host"           'Array(config.dig("servers", "web", "hosts")).size == 1'
+assert "terminates SSL at the proxy"               'config.dig("proxy", "ssl") == true'
+assert "issues the certificate for a hostname"     'config.dig("proxy", "host").to_s.length > 3'
+assert "keeps /rails/storage on a volume"          'Array(config["volumes"]).any? { |v| v.to_s.end_with?(":/rails/storage") }'
+assert "pushes to a registry"                      'config.dig("registry", "username").to_s.length > 1'
+assert "pulls the registry password from a secret" 'Array(config.dig("registry", "password")).include?("KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD")'
+assert "sets a builder arch"                       'config.dig("builder", "arch").to_s.length > 3'
+assert "bridges release assets"                    'config["asset_path"] == "/rails/public/assets"'
+assert "runs background jobs in the container"     'config.dig("env", "clear", "SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA") == "true"'
+assert "points at a mail server"                   'config.dig("env", "clear", "SMTP_ADDRESS").to_s.length > 3'
+assert "sends mail from an address"                'config.dig("env", "clear", "MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS").to_s.length > 3'
+
+for secret in SECRET_KEY_BASE SMTP_PASSWORD VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY; do
+  assert "injects $secret as a secret" "Array(config.dig('env', 'secret')).include?('$secret')"
+done
+
+if [ ! -f "$SECRETS" ]; then
+  bad ".kamal/secrets.example exists"
+else
+  # Secrets are the ones the config asks for, and the example documents each.
+  missing="$(ruby -ryaml -e '
+    config = YAML.safe_load(STDIN.read, aliases: true)
+    wanted = Array(config.dig("env", "secret")) + Array(config.dig("registry", "password"))
+    known = File.read(ARGV[0])
+    puts wanted.select { |key| known !~ /^#{key}=/m }
+  ' "$SECRETS" <<<"$rendered" 2>&1)"
+  if [ -z "$missing" ]; then
+    ok ".kamal/secrets.example documents every secret"
+  else
+    bad ".kamal/secrets.example is missing: $(echo "$missing" | tr '\n' ' ')"
+  fi
+fi
+
+# Prefer what git itself would ignore, and fall back to the rule it comes
+# from when there is no repository to ask (a tarball checkout, say).
+if git -C "$ROOT" check-ignore -q .kamal/secrets 2>/dev/null ||
+   grep -qE "^/\.kamal/secrets$" "$ROOT/.gitignore"; then
+  ok ".kamal/secrets stays out of git"
+else
+  bad ".kamal/secrets is not gitignored"
+fi
+
+if [ -f "$GUIDE" ] && grep -q "bin/kamal setup" "$GUIDE"; then
+  ok "docs/self-hosting.md walks through bin/kamal setup"
+else
+  bad "docs/self-hosting.md is missing or does not mention bin/kamal setup"
+fi
+
+if grep -q "docs/self-hosting.md" "$ROOT/README.md"; then
+  ok "README.md links the guide"
+else
+  bad "README.md does not link docs/self-hosting.md"
+fi
+
+echo
+if [ "$fail" -eq 0 ]; then
+  echo "$pass checks passed."
+else
+  echo "$fail of $((pass + fail)) checks failed." >&2
+fi
+exit "$fail"
